          "text": "---\n\n## Review: Crucible — Adversarial Multi-Agent Debate Plugin\n\nI've read every file in the project. Here's my assessment.\n\n### What this is\n\nA Claude Code plugin that runs N debater agents through structured adversarial rounds (propose → self-critique → cross-attack → defend → converge), then an Opus arbiter synthesizes the final answer. Zero runtime code — entirely Markdown-as-instructions interpreted by Claude Code.\n\n---\n\n### What's genuinely good\n\n**1. The core insight is sound.** Adversarial pressure surfaces weaknesses that a single agent will never self-identify. This is well-supported by AI safety research (Irving et al.'s \"AI safety via debate\"). The approach directly attacks the problem of confident-but-wrong outputs.\n\n**2. The phase structure is well-designed.** PROPOSE → SELF_CRITIQUE → CROSS_ATTACK → DEFEND is a smart escalation. Self-critique before cross-attack means agents arrive at the debate already hardened — opponents attack a strengthened position, not the first draft. This raises the quality floor.\n\n**3. Progressive summarization is clever.** Full text for Round 1 + digests for middle rounds + full text for the final round is a pragmatic solution to context pressure. The mandatory `### Round Digest` output contract in every defense makes this work.\n\n**4. Adaptive convergence with focus steering.** The Haiku convergence check doesn't just ask \"done?\". When it returns DIVERGED, it extracts specific `FOCUS:` areas that get injected into the next round's attack prompts. Later rounds are targeted, not generic. This is the key mechanism that prevents \"going in circles.\"\n\n**5. Tiered model economics.** Sonnet for debaters, Haiku for convergence, Opus for the arbiter. Matches capability to stakes at each stage. Smart.\n\n**6. Evidence accountability.** Debaters have `Read, Grep, Glob, WebSearch`. The `[EVIDENCE]` tagging creates a citation chain. The arbiter scores \"Evidence Usage\" as 1 of 6 criteria. This creates an incentive to ground claims rather than assert them.\n\n**7. Send-back with one-shot guard.** The arbiter can request one more round for specific gaps. `SEND_BACK_USED` prevents infinite loops. Elegant.\n\n---\n\n### Where it falls short\n\n**1. Shared-model bias — the biggest structural risk.** All debaters run the same model (Sonnet or Opus). Same training, same biases, same knowledge gaps, same reasoning patterns. When both agents make the same mistake — and they will, because they're the same model — the debate can't surface it. The arbiter prompt says \"Points both agents agreed on → high confidence\" — but agreement ≠ correctness. This is the fundamental limitation of same-model debate.\n\n*Mitigation idea:* Force genuinely different reasoning strategies per agent (e.g., one optimizes for performance, another for simplicity, another for correctness) through differentiated system prompts rather than just different names.\n\n**2. The arbiter has no tools.** (`agents/arbiter.md` declares no `allowed-tools`.) The arbiter judges code quality, correctness, and evidence usage but can't read the codebase, verify citations, or check claims independently. It's making high-stakes judgments about evidence it can only evaluate at face value. For code-heavy tasks, this is a significant blind spot.\n\n**3. Section extraction is brittle.** Lines like \"extract the `### Round Digest` section\" are natural-language parsing instructions. If a debater outputs `## Round Digest` or `**Round Digest**` or slightly reformats, the orchestrator either silently gets garbage or misses it entirely. There's no fallback or error handling — there can't be, since the orchestrator is just another LLM following Markdown instructions.\n\n**4. Cost scales quadratically.** With N agents, each cross-attack round runs N×(N-1) critiques. At N=5, that's 20 parallel agent calls per round — just for attacks. A full 5-agent, 5-round run could easily hit 80+ agent calls. The README doesn't warn about this cost curve.\n\n**5. Convergence check quality.** Using Haiku (the cheapest model) to judge whether complex technical solutions have truly converged is a real risk. A false CONVERGED exits the loop prematurely; a false DIVERGED wastes rounds. For nuanced technical disagreements, Haiku may lack the depth to distinguish \"superficially similar\" from \"genuinely converged.\"\n\n**6. Arbiter is a single point of failure.** After 20-80+ agent calls, everything funnels into one Opus call with a massive transcript. If that call hallucinates, gets confused by the long context, or misreads the debate arc, the entire process fails. There's no arbiter-level redundancy or self-check.\n\n**7. No degenerate-debate detection.** If agents converge on the wrong answer (both wrong the same way due to shared-model bias), the system has no mechanism to catch it. The arbiter will see agreement and call it high confidence.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict\n\n**Is this a good approach for achieving correct detail in agents?**\n\n**Yes — it's one of the better approaches I've seen, and the implementation is thoughtful.** The adversarial structure genuinely forces weaknesses into the open. The adaptive convergence, progressive summarization, and tiered models show real architectural thinking. For tasks where multiple valid approaches exist and debaters can bring different perspectives, this will produce substantially better answers than a single-shot query.\n\n**But it's not a silver bullet.** The shared-model bias means it can't catch errors both agents make identically. The toolless arbiter can't independently verify claims. And the quadratic cost curve means you're paying heavily for the improvement.\n\n**Three changes that would meaningfully strengthen it:**\n\n1. **Give the arbiter tools** (at least `Read, Grep, Glob`) so it can independently verify evidence and code claims\n2. **Differentiate agent personas** beyond just names — force distinct reasoning strategies (e.g., \"optimize for correctness\", \"optimize for simplicity\", \"play devil's advocate\") to reduce shared-bias risk\n3. **Upgrade the convergence check to Sonnet** — the cost saving from Haiku is small relative to the total cost, and getting convergence wrong wastes entire rounds\n\nOverall: solid work. The design is well above average for a plugin of this kind.",
